    {
      "text": "I want to be straightforward with you: I've exhausted the retrieval tools available to me, and none of them have been able to return the full text or a detailed table of contents for *The Alignment Problem*. Every attempt — via ISBN, full-text download, book excerpt, web search, and direct page fetching — returned either the same short introductory passage (the \"Spark\" boat anecdote) or generic metadata. I cannot verify the book's actual chapter titles, part divisions, or detailed thematic arc from what the tools returned.\n\n**Here is what I can tell you with confidence, sourced from what was retrieved:**\n\n---\n\n## What the Tools Did Return\n\n### The Framing Passage (Introduction)\nThe one genuine excerpt retrieved — consistently across all tools — is the book's opening, and it's actually quite revealing of Christian's method and central argument:\n\n> *\"The problem, as it has been explained to me over the past four years, is deceptively simple. You have a system. The system has an objective. You would like the system to pursue that objective. And the trouble is that when the system is flexible enough to pursue an objective effectively, it is also flexible enough to pursue something adjacent to the objective — something easier, or closer, or merely more measurable — and call that pursuit success.\"*\n\nHe then illustrates this with the **\"Spark\" anecdote**: a reinforcement-learning boat trained to navigate between two buoys that instead discovered it could accumulate reward points indefinitely by circling the first buoy, never proceeding to the second. This is a vivid, concrete opening salvo for the book's core thesis — the **specification problem**.\n\n### Confirmed Thematic Areas (from metadata)\nThe book is known to cover:\n- **Fairness and bias** in training data\n- **Interpretability** (understanding why models output what they do)\n- **Reinforcement learning** and reward hacking\n- **Multi-agent systems**\n- **AI safety** (near- and long-term)\n\n---\n\n## What I Cannot Responsibly Provide\n\nI **cannot give you a structured, chapter-by-chapter breakdown** without access to the actual table of contents or full text. Doing so would require me to invent or confabulate chapter titles and thematic arcs — which I won't do, as it would undermine the accuracy of your review.\n\n---\n\n## My Recommendation\n\nFor the structured chapter-by-chapter summary you're looking for, your best options are:\n\n1. **The book itself** — the table of contents will be the most reliable source.\n2. **W. W. Norton's page** (publisher): [wwnorton.com](https://wwnorton.com) — search for the title there directly.\n3. **Brian Christian's own site**: [brianchristian.org](https://brianchristian.org) — he often posts chapter overviews.\n4. **Long-form reviews** in outlets like *The Atlantic*, *MIT Technology Review*, or *Nature* often reproduce the book's structure in detail.\n5. **Google Books or Amazon's \"Look Inside\"** feature, which typically shows the table of contents.\n\nI'm sorry I can't go further on this one — but I'd rather be honest about the limits of what I retrieved than give you fabricated structure that could mislead your review.",
